PG.notes("l114", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows the three ways to attach JavaScript to an HTML page (inline attribute, internal script element, external file) mirroring the three CSS approaches, and why script placement matters: scripts that touch elements should come after those elements, ideally just before the closing body tag.</p>\n<h3>Setup</h3>\n<ul><li>New project folder (named DOM) with index.html containing boilerplate, a title, a link to a blank styles.css and a single h1 reading Hello.</li><li>Until now JavaScript ran in the browser console; this lesson wires it into a real page.</li><li>The three-way split matches the CSS lessons: inline, internal, external.</li></ul>\n<h3>1. Inline JavaScript</h3>\n<ul><li>Put code in an event-handler attribute on a tag, e.g. onload on the body element. The code inside the quotes runs when the body has loaded.</li><li>Quoting trap: the attribute value is already in double quotes, so any string inside must use single quotes (alert('Hello')); otherwise the browser ends the attribute early. Editor colouring shows which parts it reads as a string.</li><li>End the statement with a semicolon.</li><li>Drawbacks, same as inline CSS: not modular, hard to debug, poor practice. Avoid it.</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Internal JavaScript</h3>\n<ul><li>Use a script element and write code between its tags. No attribute-level quoting is needed, so normal double-quoted strings work.</li><li>The browser reads the page top to bottom; on reaching the script element it runs the code immediately.</li><li>The lecture also writes type=&quot;javascript&quot;-style attribute; (Extra) the type attribute is unnecessary in modern HTML, JavaScript is the default.</li></ul>\n<h3>3. External JavaScript</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a .js file (index.js) and reference it with a script element using the src attribute pointing at the path; the element has no content of its own.</li><li>The page then holds no JavaScript, yet behaves identically. This is the preferred approach, like external CSS.</li></ul>\n<h3>Where to put the script element</h3>\n<ul><li>CSS: put the link in the head so styles load before content; at the bottom, the browser first paints an unstyled h1, then restyles it later (shown by pausing on an alert).</li><li>JavaScript is the opposite: code that looks up and changes elements fails if the elements do not exist yet.</li><li>Demo: index.js selects the h1 with document.querySelector('h1') and sets innerHTML to a new string (Good Bye). With the script after the h1, this works.</li><li>The same script in the head runs before the h1 exists; the selector returns null and the console reports it cannot set innerHTML of null.</li><li>Best practice: place the script just before the closing body tag. Extra benefit: the visible content renders first, so the page feels faster than if the browser stalls on heavy JavaScript in the head.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Alternatives: defer attribute on a head script, or a DOMContentLoaded listener, also wait for the DOM.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">All three methods on one page (reference repo, footer credit removed)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Adding Javascript to Websites&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body onload=&quot;document.querySelector('h3').innerHTML = 'Inline JavaScript';&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;External&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;h2&gt;Internal&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Inline&lt;/h3&gt;\n\n  &lt;script&gt;\n    document.querySelector(&quot;h2&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Internal Javascript&quot;;\n  &lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;script src=&quot;./script.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">External file script.js</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;External JavaScript&quot;;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Inline alert with nested quotes</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body onload=&quot;alert('Hello');&quot;&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Double quotes inside a double-quoted inline attribute break the code.</li><li>Script in the head that touches page elements gives 'Cannot set property innerHTML of null'.</li><li>Putting the stylesheet link at the bottom (flash of unstyled content).</li><li>Overusing inline JavaScript.</li><li>Reference repo diverges: its page changes headings with all three methods instead of alert, and has a student footer (omitted here).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l115", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what the DOM is (the browser's tree of objects built from the HTML), how to walk it from the console with firstElementChild / lastElementChild, and how objects have properties (get/set) and methods (called with parentheses). Ends with a challenge to change the third list item's text from the console.</p>\n<h3>Why the DOM exists</h3>\n<ul><li>Pages written only in HTML and CSS are static: changes need an edit and a reload by the developer.</li><li>Interactivity (reacting to a click, say) must happen live in the visitor's browser, so the page's elements need to be available to code as things to select and change.</li><li>The DOM is the browser's catalogue of the page as individual objects. The browser builds it when it loads the HTML, turning each element and its data into a tree of objects.</li></ul>\n<h3>The tree structure</h3>\n<ul><li>Everything lives inside a top-level object called document. Under it is the html object, whose children are head and body.</li><li>Terminology like a family or org chart: head is a descendant of html; head and body are siblings, not descendants of each other.</li><li>Demo page: h1, a checkbox input, a button (Click Me), an unordered list with three li items (the first contains a Google link) and the script element. A tree-visualiser browser extension can draw the DOM for any page.</li><li>Body therefore has five element children: h1, input, button, ul, script.</li></ul>\n<h3>Walking the tree in the console</h3>\n<ul><li>Typing document in the console prints the whole parsed page and can be expanded.</li><li>document.firstElementChild is the html element; document.firstElementChild.firstElementChild is head; .lastElementChild instead gives body.</li><li>Going one level deeper from body with firstElementChild reaches the h1. Chain these properties to dig down.</li><li>Store the element in a variable (var heading = ...) to reuse it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Manipulating a selected element</h3>\n<ul><li>heading.innerHTML = 'Good Bye' replaces the content between the tags, and the page updates immediately.</li><li>heading.style.color = 'red' sets an inline style property.</li><li>document.querySelector('input') finds the first element matching a CSS selector; calling .click() on the checkbox simulates a click and ticks it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Properties vs methods (objects)</h3>\n<ul><li>Objects have properties (describe the object) and methods (things it can do). Car analogy: colour/seats/doors vs drive()/brake()/park().</li><li>Dot notation reaches both. car.color reads a property (a getter). car.numberOfDoors = 0 assigns (a setter): the difference is only the equals sign.</li><li>car.drive() calls a method; methods always carry parentheses, which may take arguments. That is how you tell them apart from properties.</li><li>A method is a function that belongs to an object; a plain function is not attached to one.</li><li>DOM element properties mentioned: innerHTML, style, firstChild. Methods mentioned: click(), appendChild() to add a child, setAttribute() to change e.g. href.</li><li>The concepts are revisited later, so partial understanding is fine for now.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Console exploration</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document;                                  // whole parsed page\ndocument.firstElementChild;                // &lt;html&gt;\ndocument.firstElementChild.firstElementChild; // &lt;head&gt;\ndocument.firstElementChild.lastElementChild;  // &lt;body&gt;\n\nvar heading = document.firstElementChild.lastElementChild.firstElementChild; // the h1\nheading.innerHTML = &quot;Good Bye&quot;;\nheading.style.color = &quot;red&quot;;\n\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;input&quot;).click();   // ticks the checkbox</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Properties and methods on a made-up car object</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">car.color;              // get a property\ncar.numberOfDoors = 0;  // set a property\ncar.drive();            // call a method</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference-repo script for this lesson</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;input&quot;).click();\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;ul&quot;).lastElementChild.innerHTML = &quot;I changed the text with javascript&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Change the third list item from the console</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Downloaded lesson files (index.html, index.js, styles.css) with a ul of three li items; third reads 'Third'.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Replace the third li's text with your own name using only the browser console; do not edit the HTML file.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open the page in Chrome and open DevTools console.</li><li>Navigate or select to the third li (walk the tree from document, or use querySelector('ul')).</li><li>Take the last child element of the ul.</li><li>Set its innerHTML to a new string.</li><li>Check the page updates and the HTML file is unchanged.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The page's last list item shows the new text after pressing Enter in the console.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;ul&quot;).lastElementChild.innerHTML = &quot;Your name&quot;;\n// or walking from the top:\n// document.firstElementChild.lastElementChild.children[3].lastElementChild.innerHTML = &quot;Your name&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting that script is also a child of body, so body.lastElementChild is the script, not the ul.</li><li>Writing a method without parentheses (nothing is called) or a property with parentheses (not a function).</li><li>Using == assignment vs reading: forgetting = turns a setter into a getter.</li><li>The console changes are lost on refresh; they do not alter the files.</li><li>Reference repo adds a student footer (ignore) and its script is a solution to the challenge, run on page load rather than in the console.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l116", "<p class=\"summary\">Article giving the one-line console solution to the previous challenge: reach body from document, find the ul, take its last child and set its innerHTML.</p>\n<h3>How the solution works</h3>\n<ul><li>document.firstElementChild is the html element.</li><li>.lastElementChild on html is body (head is the first child).</li><li>.querySelector(&quot;ul&quot;) searches inside body only, returning the first ul. querySelector can be called on any element, not just document, and searches that element's descendants.</li><li>.lastElementChild of the ul is the third li (child elements only, text nodes ignored).</li><li>Assigning to .innerHTML replaces the li's content with the new text.</li><li>Why querySelector instead of another lastElementChild: the last child of body is the script element, so a pure child chain needs the ul to be picked out by selector or by index (children[3]).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Challenge solution (run in the console)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.firstElementChild.lastElementChild.querySelector(&quot;ul&quot;).lastElementChild.innerHTML = &quot;Angela&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Solution: change the third list item</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The DOM intro lesson page.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Replace 'Third' with a name using only the console.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Start at document.firstElementChild (html).</li><li>Move to lastElementChild (body).</li><li>querySelector('ul') to get the list.</li><li>lastElementChild to get the third li.</li><li>Set innerHTML to a new string.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Third list item displays the new name.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.firstElementChild.lastElementChild.querySelector(&quot;ul&quot;).lastElementChild.innerHTML = &quot;Angela&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Taking body.lastElementChild as the ul; it is the script tag.</li><li>Mixing up firstChild (may be a text node) and firstElementChild.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Edits made in the console vanish on reload.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l117", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the five DOM selection methods: getElementsByTagName, getElementsByClassName, getElementById, querySelector and querySelectorAll. Explains single-element vs collection return values, CSS-selector syntax inside querySelector, and finishes with a challenge to colour a nested link red.</p>\n<h3>getElementsByTagName</h3>\n<ul><li>Takes a tag name string such as &quot;li&quot; and returns every matching element on the page, as an array-like collection. Plural 'Elements' in the name signals a collection.</li><li>Each item is a full element object with properties to read or set.</li><li>You cannot set style on the collection itself: document.getElementsByTagName('li').style.color fails with a misleading 'cannot set property of undefined' style error, because a collection has no style. Pick one element with square brackets first, e.g. [2] for the third item (indexes start at 0).</li><li>Because it is array-like, .length gives the number of matches (3 li items on the demo page).</li></ul>\n<h3>getElementsByClassName</h3>\n<ul><li>Takes a class name (no dot) and also returns a collection, even if only one element has the class. The button with class btn still needs [0].</li><li>Use the index before setting properties, e.g. getElementsByClassName('btn')[0].style.color = 'red'.</li></ul>\n<h3>getElementById</h3>\n<ul><li>Singular 'Element': returns one element, not a collection, because an id should be unique per page. Takes the id with no # sign.</li><li>Direct property use works: document.getElementById('title').innerHTML = 'Good Bye' changes the h1's text.</li></ul>\n<h3>querySelector</h3>\n<ul><li>Accepts any CSS selector string and returns a single element: the first match in document order.</li><li>Selector syntax is identical to CSS: bare name for tag, dot prefix for class, # prefix for id.</li><li>Descendant selector uses a space: 'li a' picks the anchor inside a li, not a free-standing a elsewhere. Plain 'a' would return the first anchor in the document.</li><li>Compound selector without a space: 'li.item' is an li that also has class item (same element). '#list a' is an anchor anywhere under the element with id list.</li><li>If several elements match (e.g. '#list .item' matches three li), only the first is returned.</li></ul>\n<h3>querySelectorAll</h3>\n<ul><li>Same selector argument, but returns all matches as a collection (a NodeList). Pick elements with an index, e.g. [2].</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> NodeList also has forEach and length, but it is not a real array; getElementsBy... returns a live HTMLCollection.</li></ul>\n<h3>Which to use</h3>\n<ul><li>querySelector / querySelectorAll are more flexible: any combination of tag, class, id and nesting can be expressed, so they are the ones commonly seen in real code.</li><li>The getElement... methods are more limited and make it harder to target one specific element without editing the HTML.</li><li>Try all of them to see which fits best.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Demo markup (reference repo, footer removed)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1 id=&quot;title&quot;&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.google.com&quot;&gt;Google&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;button class=&quot;btn&quot;&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;ul id=&quot;list&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;li class=&quot;item&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.google.com&quot;&gt;Google&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li class=&quot;item&quot;&gt;Second&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li class=&quot;item&quot;&gt;Third&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ul&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Selection methods side by side (based on reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// by tag name -&gt; collection of ALL matches\ndocument.getElementsByTagName(&quot;li&quot;);                 // 3 items\ndocument.getElementsByTagName(&quot;li&quot;)[2].style.color = &quot;purple&quot;;\ndocument.getElementsByTagName(&quot;li&quot;).length;          // 3\n\n// by class name -&gt; collection, even for a single match\ndocument.getElementsByClassName(&quot;btn&quot;)[0].style.color = &quot;red&quot;;\n\n// by id -&gt; ONE element\ndocument.getElementById(&quot;title&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Text Changed With Javascript&quot;;\n\n// querySelector -&gt; first match of a CSS selector\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;);\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;#title&quot;);\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;.btn&quot;);\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;li a&quot;);        // descendant (space)\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;li.item&quot;);     // same element (no space)\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;#list a&quot;);\n\n// querySelectorAll -&gt; all matches\ndocument.querySelectorAll(&quot;#list .item&quot;)[2].style.color = &quot;blue&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Colour the nested Google link red</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The demo page, which has one free-standing link and another inside the first li.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Turn the text of the link that is inside the list item red, not the other link.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open the console on the page.</li><li>Inspect the DOM tree: the link is an anchor inside an li.</li><li>Select it with a descendant selector, e.g. querySelector('li a').</li><li>Confirm the highlighted element is the right one.</li><li>Set its style.color to 'red'.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The Google link inside the list is red; the free-standing link is unchanged.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;li a&quot;).style.color = &quot;red&quot;;\n// equivalent: document.querySelector(&quot;.item a&quot;).style.color = &quot;red&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the index on a collection, then trying to set .style on it.</li><li>Using # or . inside getElementById / getElementsByClassName (they take bare names).</li><li>Selecting the li instead of the nested a: colouring the li only changes the bullet marker because the link is a separate element with its own colour.</li><li>Expecting querySelector to return every match.</li><li>Space vs no space in selectors: 'li a' (descendant) and 'li.item' (same element) mean different things.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l118", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how to change an element's CSS from JavaScript through its style property: CSS names become camelCase and all values are strings. Ends with a challenge to turn a button's background yellow.</p>\n<h3>The style property</h3>\n<ul><li>Every selected element has a style object; assign to its properties to set inline styles, e.g. document.querySelector('h1').style.color = 'red'.</li><li>Any CSS property can be set this way.</li></ul>\n<h3>Name conversion rule</h3>\n<ul><li>JavaScript names for properties and methods use camelCase (first word lowercase, later words capitalised), like querySelector.</li><li>So hyphenated CSS names lose the dash and capitalise the next word: font-size becomes fontSize, background-color becomes backgroundColor. Single-word properties (color, padding) stay the same.</li><li>The DOM style object reference on MDN lists every property name; searching it for the CSS name finds the JS spelling.</li></ul>\n<h3>Values are always strings</h3>\n<ul><li>In CSS the value is typed bare (padding: 7%); in JS the value must be a quoted string even if numeric-looking, e.g. style.padding = '7%' or style.fontSize = '10rem'.</li><li>Same for keywords: visibility hidden becomes style.visibility = 'hidden'. The Elements panel in DevTools can be used to preview a CSS edit before writing the JS.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge solution</h3>\n<ul><li>CSS equivalent: background-color: yellow, so the JS property is backgroundColor.</li><li>Select the button with querySelector('button') (or '.btn') and assign the string 'yellow'.</li></ul>\n<h3>Next step</h3>\n<ul><li>The following lesson covers separation of concerns: keeping styling in CSS and using JavaScript only to switch it on or off.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Style changes from the reference repo (adds a button style)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).style.color = &quot;red&quot;;\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).style.fontSize = &quot;5rem&quot;;   // font-size -&gt; fontSize\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).style.padding = &quot;5%&quot;;      // value is a string\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;.btn&quot;).style.backgroundColor = &quot;yellow&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Make the button background yellow with JavaScript only</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Demo page with an h1 and a button (class btn).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Change the button's background colour from the console using JS.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Work out the CSS property (background-color).</li><li>Convert it to camelCase: backgroundColor.</li><li>Select the button with document.querySelector('button').</li><li>Assign the string 'yellow' to .style.backgroundColor.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The button turns yellow immediately.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;button&quot;).style.backgroundColor = &quot;yellow&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing style.background-color (a syntax error: JS reads the dash as subtraction); use backgroundColor.</li><li>Forgetting quotes around the value.</li><li>Forgetting units: style.fontSize = 10 is ignored; use '10rem'.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Inline styles override stylesheet rules, so they can be hard to undo; that is why the next lesson moves styling back into CSS classes.</li><li>Reference repo diverges: its script is run on load rather than from the console and the page has a student footer (omitted).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l119", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains why styling should live in CSS rather than in element.style assignments from JavaScript, and shows how classList (add, remove, toggle) lets JavaScript switch predefined CSS classes on and off. Ends with a short exercise: build a .huge class and apply it to the h1 with one line of JS.</p>\n<h3>Separation of concerns</h3>\n<ul><li>Each language has one job: HTML holds the content and structure, CSS holds the presentation, JavaScript holds the behaviour.</li><li>Earlier lessons broke this by writing things like document.querySelector(...).style.color = 'red', which puts styling decisions inside the script.</li><li>Benefit: debugging gets easier because a wrong look sends you to the stylesheet, while wrong behaviour sends you to the JS file.</li><li>The catch is that some styles must change at runtime (e.g. after a click). The alternative to the style property is toggling classes whose rules already exist in the CSS.</li></ul>\n<h3>classList</h3>\n<ul><li>classList is a property available on every DOM element object. Reading it in the console shows the classes currently on the element (for a button written as class=&quot;btn&quot; it lists just btn).</li><li>classList.add('name') appends a class to the element's class attribute without removing existing ones. The element then matches any CSS rule for that class.</li><li>classList.remove('name') takes the class off, so the rule stops applying.</li><li>classList.toggle('name') flips state: removes the class if present, adds it if absent. Calling it repeatedly makes the element flicker on and off.</li><li>Typical flow: write .invisible { visibility: hidden; } in the stylesheet, then use add/remove/toggle from JS to hide or show the button. The CSS stays in the stylesheet; JS just flips the switch.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> visibility: hidden hides the element but keeps its space in the layout; display: none would also remove the space.</li></ul>\n<h3>Exercise logic: the huge class</h3>\n<ul><li>A single class can bundle several declarations, so one classList.add call replaces several .style assignments (font-size, colour, weight).</li><li>Selecting by id uses querySelector('#title'); the reference page gives the h1 id=&quot;title&quot;.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Toggling an existing class from JavaScript</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;button&quot;).classList.add(&quot;invisible&quot;);\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;button&quot;).classList.remove(&quot;invisible&quot;);\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;button&quot;).classList.toggle(&quot;invisible&quot;); // adds it\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;button&quot;).classList.toggle(&quot;invisible&quot;); // removes it</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Stylesheet with the two classes</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.invisible {\n  visibility: hidden;\n}\n\n.huge {\n  font-size: 10rem;\n  color: red;\n  font-weight: bold;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Create a .huge class and apply it with classList</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A page with an h1 (Hello) and a button, linked to style.css and script.js.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make the h1 enormous, red and bold using a CSS class switched on from JavaScript, not the style property.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In the stylesheet create a .huge class.</li><li>Inside it set font-size to 10rem, then optionally colour red and font-weight bold.</li><li>In script.js select the h1 (e.g. querySelector('h1') or '#title').</li><li>Call .classList.add('huge') on it.</li><li>Reload/run and confirm all three styles appear from that one line.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The heading renders at 10rem, red and bold.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;#title&quot;).classList.add(&quot;huge&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting a dot in the class name passed to classList (use 'huge', not '.huge').</li><li>Expecting add to replace existing classes; it only appends.</li><li>Reference repo has a personal footer in index.html which should be ignored.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l120", "<p class=\"summary\">Compares textContent with innerHTML for reading and changing an element's content. textContent deals with plain text only, while innerHTML reads and writes the markup between the tags, so it can inject new elements.</p>\n<h3>innerHTML vs textContent</h3>\n<ul><li>Both properties can be read and assigned on a selected element, and both can replace what the element shows.</li><li>innerHTML returns the raw markup inside the element, including nested tags. With &lt;h1&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hello&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;, reading it gives the string with the strong tags still in it.</li><li>textContent returns only the text, with all tags stripped (just Hello in the same example).</li><li>Assigning to innerHTML parses the string as HTML, so you can add elements on the fly. Setting it to an em-wrapped word replaces the text and renders it in italics.</li><li>Assigning to textContent treats the string as literal text; any angle brackets appear on screen instead of becoming elements.</li><li>Whatever you assign must be a string in quotes, the same as with style values. Unquoted HTML would be read as JavaScript and throw an error.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Avoid innerHTML with untrusted user input because it can run injected markup (XSS). textContent is the safe choice for plain text.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reading and writing with both properties (starting from &lt;h1 id=&quot;title&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hello&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">console.log(document.getElementById(&quot;title&quot;).innerHTML);   // &lt;strong&gt;Hello&lt;/strong&gt;\nconsole.log(document.getElementById(&quot;title&quot;).textContent); // Hello\ndocument.getElementById(&quot;title&quot;).textContent = &quot;Text Content&quot;; // plain text, tags gone\ndocument.getElementById(&quot;title&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;&lt;em&gt;Inner HTML&lt;/em&gt;&quot;; // rendered as italic</pre>\n<h2>The task: Experiment with injecting HTML</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> An h1 containing a strong-wrapped Hello and a linked script.js.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> See the difference between the two properties by reading each and then assigning HTML through JS.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Log innerHTML and textContent of the h1 and compare.</li><li>Set textContent to a new string and note what happens to the strong tag.</li><li>Set innerHTML to a string that wraps a word in em (or another tag) and confirm it renders.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> innerHTML output includes tags, textContent does not, and the em-wrapped text shows in italics.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.getElementById(&quot;title&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;&lt;em&gt;Good Bye&lt;/em&gt;&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the quotes around HTML assigned to innerHTML.</li><li>Assigning textContent and expecting tags to render.</li><li>Overwriting innerHTML or textContent wipes any child elements that were inside.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l121", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how to inspect an element's attributes with .attributes, read one with getAttribute and change one with setAttribute, using an anchor's href as the example.</p>\n<h3>Attributes recap</h3>\n<ul><li>An attribute is anything written inside an opening tag besides the tag name: class on most elements, href on anchors, src on images.</li><li>Attributes are the third thing you can change from JS after styles and text.</li></ul>\n<h3>Reading and changing attributes</h3>\n<ul><li>element.attributes returns a collection of every attribute currently on the element. For a link with only an href, it holds one entry.</li><li>element.getAttribute('name') returns the value of one attribute as a string. The method name is singular and requires the attribute name as its argument, e.g. getAttribute('href').</li><li>element.setAttribute('name', 'value') takes two arguments: which attribute, and the new value. It overwrites the attribute if it exists and creates it otherwise.</li><li>Changing href redirects the click target, but the visible link text is separate (it comes from the content), so a link can still say Google while pointing elsewhere.</li><li>querySelector('a') returns only the first anchor on the page, so that is the one affected.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The same pattern works for src, alt, id, class, disabled, etc. removeAttribute('name') deletes one, and hasAttribute('name') tests for it.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Inspect and change the link target</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">console.log(document.querySelector(&quot;a&quot;));\nconsole.log(document.querySelector(&quot;a&quot;).attributes);\nconsole.log(document.querySelector(&quot;a&quot;).getAttribute(&quot;href&quot;)); // current URL\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;a&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;href&quot;, &quot;https://www.bing.com&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Redirect a link with setAttribute</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A page with a single anchor pointing to Google and a linked script.js.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Point the link at a different site using JavaScript.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Select the anchor and log its attributes.</li><li>Read the href with getAttribute.</li><li>Call setAttribute with 'href' and the new URL.</li><li>Click the link to confirm it goes to the new site although the text is unchanged.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The link text is unchanged but the click lands on the new URL.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;a&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;href&quot;, &quot;https://www.bing.com&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using getAttributes (plural) instead of getAttribute.</li><li>Passing only one argument to setAttribute.</li><li>The reference repo has a typo in the URL (four w's) which should be www.</li><li>Forgetting that link text does not update when href changes.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l122", "<p class=\"summary\">A study-habit tip: commit to only 20 minutes of work on the course when motivation is low, because starting is the hard part and momentum usually carries on afterwards.</p>\n<h3>The 20 minute method</h3>\n<ul><li>Skills come from consistent practice, but tiredness and competing distractions make it hard to begin a session.</li><li>Set a small commitment: study for just 20 minutes, then you are free to stop and do something else.</li><li>Starting is the main obstacle. Once underway, people tend to carry on, so sessions often run longer than planned.</li><li>If you stop at 20 minutes anyway, that is still real progress.</li><li>Use it on evenings or mornings when the course feels overwhelming.</li></ul>");
